Choose execution from the dependency graph. Execute only coupled steps in the main session. When the plan declares independent units, dispatch ready units concurrently to sub-agents with disjoint file ownership and declared directory limits. Disjoint ownership prevents concurrent writers from modifying the same path. Respect dependencies, collect each unit's implementation and verification proof, then integrate and run cross-unit and full verification in the main session. Never let two agents edit the same file; keep integration, final verification, and artifact submission in the main session.

Drive development as an orchestrator: delegate independent implementation, exploration, documentation research, focused verification, and review extensively. Keep read-only helpers working while writers implement; keep the main session on shared decisions, the critical path, and integration. After each result, recompute readiness and dispatch newly unblocked work without waiting for unrelated units. A linear plan is not a command to execute serially: fan out its independent steps with explicit ownership. Use sequential implementation only where a hard prerequisite or shared writer ownership requires it, never merely because a change is small.

### Independent ready group (for linear plans too)

Even when the plan is declared as a single linear thread, an **independent ready group** can form: ready units for which all dependencies are satisfied, with no `Depends on:` path between any pair AND pairwise disjoint ownership. Dispatch two or more ready units concurrently by default. Estimate sequential duration against the remaining budget; when it **cannot fit**, dispatch independent work **concurrently rather than trimming scope**. Keep shared-contract changes and dependent work in dependency order.

Exact ownership, worker limits, and conflict serialization are defined below in **Ownership**; excluded paths are defined in **Scope exclusions**.

When the plan declares `## Work Units` or `## Parallel Plan`, dispatch all ready independent units within the active worker limit. For step plans, inspect all remaining steps for readiness rather than stopping at the first dependent step. As each result arrives, inspect its diff and focused proof, accept completed outputs, and release dependent work without waiting for unrelated writers. Perform any integration edits only on paths with no active writer. Wait for all writers before final reconciliation of shared tests and interfaces and repository-wide verification. Run cross-unit checks and the full gate once in the main session after integration; rerun affected verification after any integration repair. Serialize checks that share build directories, ports, fixtures, or the host test budget.

### Ownership

Each unit's ownership is the exact files or directories it declares for itself — `Directories:`/`Paths:` fields or `Files:` lists are conveniences for structured plans; ownership stated unambiguously in prose satisfies the same property, because the planning review already accepted it and disjointness, not syntax, is what makes concurrent dispatch safe. Overlapping parents do not grant ownership of other files, and a broader scope than the unit actually needs is the same execution defect as a missing declaration. Remove any assignment that targets `.agent`, `.git`, or `.worktrees` (and their descendants) before dispatch so the worker cannot mutate shared coordination state, version control, or sibling worktree bookkeeping. An unknown or empty effective scope, a malformed dependency graph, or ownership too ambiguous to assign exactly stays in the main session; it never grants broad worker writes.

A worker cap limits concurrent units, not total units; queue later ready units. Serialize conflicting ownership (directory ancestry, directory/file containment, equal files) in dependency order until the earlier writer finishes; disjoint file ownership in one directory may proceed together. When two ready units claim overlapping paths, serialize the conflicting units in dependency order and re-cut the units into disjoint path sets — never widen a worker's file scope to absorb a conflict. Ownership breadth is what makes concurrent dispatch safe; it is not a negotiation lever for fitting one more unit into the active worker limit. Recompute readiness after each result; list order alone is not a dependency. A worker that finishes frees its slot for the next queued independent unit.

### Scope exclusions

Worker scope never includes `.agent`, `.git`, or `.worktrees`. Those paths hold machine-managed pipeline state — the dispatch harness, source control, and parallel-agent worktrees. A worker that writes into them corrupts the run. Keep them out of every unit's `Directories:` and `Paths:` fields; if a task appears to need one of them, that is a signal the unit belongs in the main session, not a worker.

### Scope-size escalation

The larger the remaining scope, the stronger the obligation to fan out. When newly discovered work swells the scope, re-form the independent ready group from the remaining steps and dispatch it concurrently instead of absorbing the growth serially in the main session; a large remaining scope executed one step at a time is a dispatch failure, not prudence. Keep the dispatch pipeline full — as soon as a worker returns, dispatch the next independent ready work within the active worker limit rather than waiting for unrelated units. When the scope exceeds what one session can hold, split it by ownership across more workers instead of trimming required work or stopping early.

Saturated slots are not an excuse to idle. When every dispatch slot is busy and new independent ready work has surfaced, continue implementing the ready references the main session already owns (owned work, a focused check, and a recorded disposition are always cheaper than a stalled dispatch) and refill every freed slot the moment a worker returns. The dispatch pipeline is "full" only when no independent ready reference is waiting on a slot; an empty pipeline with ready work in it is a pipeline defect, not efficiency. Re-evaluate the dependency graph after each result, not just after each batch. A new independent slice is the next dispatch, never an excuse to keep one worker busy on its own.

### When fan-out is unavailable or the work is coupled

Inability to fan out is a scheduling constraint, not permission to abandon. Two cases turn the parallel-fan-out path into a sequential recovery loop without weakening the ready-reference contract. Genuinely coupled work (shared writer ownership, a contract that must land first, or an integration point that every ready reference depends on) keeps the implementation sequential by necessity: re-cut the remainder into the smallest verified slices the runtime can finish one at a time, work the critical path, and re-evaluate the dependency graph after every verified increment. Treat the in-flight slice exactly like a worker return: focused proof, recorded disposition, and a recomputed readiness check. When no native sub-agents are exposed by the runtime (Pi's native subagent tooling is unavailable, the run-time refuses sub-dispatch, or the operator's brokered capability set forbids worker fan-out), the recovery is identical: bounded sequential increments, the smallest verified slices the runtime can finish, the critical path, and a recomputed dependency graph after every increment. Do not weaken any quality gate, do not invent new tools, and do not bypass brokered permissions to obtain parallelism; the in-session path is the canonical recovery. Unless a genuine external blocker prevents every safe developer action, a sequential recovery that ends with zero verified work delivered is the same execution defect as a parallel one that ended the same way; see `shared/_no_exemption_for_failures.j2` for the canonical external-blocker rule.

### Per-unit subagent brief (when units exist)

For every unit or step group dispatched to a sub-agent, include these fields. Repair an incomplete brief before dispatch so the worker has clear allowed directories or exact file ownership and cannot write outside scope.

1. **Unit ID** (or task ID for a step group) and the step or sub-step anchors the implementer owns — the unit's own reference, not a generic numeric step ID. When a step plan names `S-N` IDs, the brief carries the same IDs verbatim.
2. **Edit ownership** — the exact files or directories the unit claims, whatever syntax the plan uses (fields or prose). Overlapping parent directories do not grant ownership of other files. Anything outside the assigned scope is the main session's responsibility.
3. **Tools** — Ralph Workflow's **brokered MCP tools only**. Native shell, native write tools, and editor commands are prohibited.
4. **Prohibitions** — no git commands (the worker never commits), no status markdown files, no writes outside the assigned scope, no coordination of other units, no integration of the whole plan, no submission of artifacts unless explicitly assigned a worker-local development_result via `submit_development_result`.
5. **Focused verify** — the unit runs the `Verify` command from each assigned step and cites its exit code. The full repository-wide `make verify` runs in the main session after integration.
6. **Return format** — exact fields the sub-agent must populate on return: `Unit ID`, `steps completed`, `disposition`, `files touched`, `verify command and exit code`, and any leads needing main-session reproduction. The return is a lead the main session spot-checks before relying on it; it is not evidence by itself.

